    { key: 'vis', icon: '🌈', label: 'Visible', title: 'Visible Light (380–700 nm)', body: 'The visible range is a tiny sliver of the spectrum — about 1 octave in frequency, from violet (380nm, ~3.1eV) to red (700nm, ~1.8eV). Our eyes evolved to detect this range because it\'s where the Sun\'s blackbody emission peaks. Photosynthesis uses 400–700nm. The retina has three types of cone cells sensitive to roughly red, green, and blue, and the brain combines these to produce colour perception. Most of the information you have ever received about the world arrived as visible photons.' },
    { key: 'uv', icon: '☀️', label: 'UV', title: 'Ultraviolet (10–380 nm)', body: 'Photon energies of 3–120 eV are enough to break chemical bonds and ionise atoms — which is why UV causes sunburn and DNA damage (specifically pyrimidine dimer formation). The ozone layer (O₃) absorbs most solar UV-C (100–280nm) and UV-B (280–315nm). UV-A (315–400nm) reaches the surface and penetrates skin more deeply. Blacklights emit UV-A and cause fluorescence. Many insects, birds, and bees can see UV; flower patterns invisible to humans are highly visible to pollinators.' },
