    { key: 'eh', icon: '⚫', label: 'Event Horizon', title: 'Event Horizon — the point of no return', body: 'The event horizon is not a physical surface — it is the boundary in spacetime beyond which the escape velocity exceeds c. For a non-rotating black hole, it is a sphere of radius r_s = 2GM/c² (the Schwarzschild radius). For the Sun\'s mass, r_s ≈ 3 km. For Earth, it\'s 9mm. Nothing that crosses the event horizon can return, including light — but an infalling observer feels nothing special at the crossing. From the outside, however, light from the infalling observer is infinitely redshifted: the observer appears to freeze and fade.' },
    { key: 'hawk', icon: '🌡️', label: 'Hawking Radiation', title: 'Hawking Radiation — black holes aren\'t black', body: 'Stephen Hawking predicted in 1974 that black holes emit thermal radiation due to quantum effects near the event horizon. Virtual particle-antiparticle pairs constantly appear from the vacuum; when they form near the horizon, one can fall in while the other escapes. To conserve energy, the black hole loses mass. The temperature is T = ℏc³/(8πGMk_B) — inversely proportional to mass. Stellar-mass black holes have temperatures of ~10⁻⁸ K, far too cold to detect. Primordial black holes of ~10¹²kg would be evaporating now. Hawking radiation has never been directly observed but is considered theoretically robust.' },
    { key: 'spag', icon: '🍝', label: 'Spaghettification', title: 'Spaghettification — tidal forces at the horizon', body: 'Near a black hole, tidal forces become extreme: gravity is much stronger on the side of your body facing the hole than on the far side, so you are stretched radially and compressed laterally — spaghettified. For stellar-mass black holes (r_s ≈ 3km), tidal forces become fatal well outside the horizon. For supermassive black holes (r_s ≈ millions of km), the tidal forces at the horizon are actually manageable — you could cross the event horizon of a large enough black hole without being torn apart immediately. You\'d be trapped and eventually destroyed as you fell deeper, but the horizon itself would be unremarkable.' },
