    { key: 'ent', icon: '🔗', label: 'Entanglement', title: 'Quantum Entanglement', body: 'Two particles can be correlated in such a way that measuring one instantly determines the state of the other, regardless of distance. Einstein called this "spooky action at a distance" and believed it proved QM was incomplete (EPR paradox, 1935). John Bell (1964) showed that any local hidden variable theory must satisfy the Bell inequality. Experiments by Aspect (1982) and many successors — including loophole-free tests (Hensen et al., 2015) — consistently violate Bell inequalities, confirming the universe is genuinely non-local at the quantum level. Entanglement cannot transmit information but enables quantum teleportation and quantum key distribution.' },
