    { key: 'zeno', icon: '👁️', label: 'Quantum Zeno', title: 'Quantum Zeno Effect', body: 'Measuring a quantum system repeatedly can freeze its evolution — "the watched pot never boils." If you measure whether a quantum system is in its initial state frequently enough, the wavefunction collapses back each time and the transition to another state is suppressed. First proposed by Misra and Sudarshan (1977). It has been experimentally confirmed using ultracold atoms and quantum optical systems. The inverse (anti-Zeno effect) can accelerate decay. This is not paradoxical — it follows directly from the measurement postulate — but it is startling: observation physically intervenes in reality.' },
    { key: 'eraser', icon: '🗑️', label: 'Quantum Eraser', title: 'Quantum Eraser', body: 'If you detect which path a particle takes in a double-slit experiment, interference disappears. But if you subsequently "erase" the which-path information before detecting the particle, interference can be restored. In the delayed-choice quantum eraser (Kim et al., 1999), the erasure decision can be made after the particle has already been detected at the screen — apparently retroactively determining whether interference occurred. This is deeply unsettling if you believe in a definite timeline. The resolution is careful analysis of correlations: you can only see the interference when you select the subset of particles for which the path information was erased.' },
    { key: 'elitzur', icon: '💣', label: 'Interaction-Free', title: 'Elitzur-Vaidman Bomb Problem', body: 'A thought experiment (1993): a bomb is triggered by a single photon. Can you detect whether it\'s live without setting it off? Using quantum interference, you can in some cases determine a bomb is live without the photon ever interacting with it — interaction-free measurement. In a Mach-Zehnder interferometer, the presence of the bomb (which would absorb a photon) changes the interference pattern even for photons that take the other path. This was experimentally realised using beam splitters and single photons. It demonstrates that quantum measurements can extract information about a system without any classical interaction.' }
