  // INTERPRETATIONS EXPLORER
  makeExplorer('interp-btns', 'interp-panel', {
    copenhagen: { label: 'Copenhagen', title: 'Copenhagen Interpretation', body: 'Developed by Bohr and Heisenberg in the 1920s, Copenhagen says the wavefunction doesn\'t represent physical reality — it encodes our knowledge of a system. Physical quantities don\'t have values until measured; measurement causes collapse. "Shut up and calculate" is its caricature. It\'s deliberately silent on what constitutes a measurement and what happens during collapse — Bohr called these questions meaningless. Most working physicists use Copenhagen operationally, but few defend it philosophically. Its strength is pragmatic precision; its weakness is refusing to answer "but what\'s really happening?"', note: 'Dominant view in textbooks; philosophically contested.' },
    mw: { label: 'Many-Worlds', title: 'Many-Worlds Interpretation (Everett, 1957)', body: 'Hugh Everett III proposed that the Schrödinger equation always evolves deterministically with no collapse. Instead, every measurement causes the universe to "branch" — both outcomes occur in different branches of a single quantum universe. There is one universal wavefunction. The branches decohere and can\'t interact (explaining why we see definite outcomes). Many physicists find this attractive because it adds nothing to standard QM — but it implies an unimaginable proliferation of parallel worlds. The hardest problem is the Born rule: why should probabilities be |ψ|² in a theory where all outcomes occur?', note: 'Popular among physicists; requires a branching multiverse.' },
    pilot: { label: 'Pilot Wave', title: 'De Broglie-Bohm Pilot Wave Theory', body: 'Particles have definite positions at all times — they are guided by a "pilot wave" (the wavefunction) through configuration space. The theory is deterministic and produces exactly the same predictions as standard QM. Randomness enters only because we don\'t know the exact initial conditions (a hidden variable). Einstein would have approved. The catch: the guidance is non-local — a particle can be influenced instantaneously by conditions far away. This non-locality is compatible with special relativity (you still can\'t use it for FTL signalling), but many physicists find it aesthetically troubling. Bell himself favoured pilot wave theory.', note: 'Fully deterministic; non-local; same predictions as standard QM.' },
    qbism: { label: 'QBism', title: 'QBism (Quantum Bayesianism)', body: 'QBism (Fuchs, Mermin, Schack) treats the wavefunction as an agent\'s subjective beliefs about future experiences — like a Bayesian probability distribution. Measurement doesn\'t collapse anything "out there"; it updates the agent\'s beliefs. The Born rule is a normative consistency constraint on betting, not a physical law. QBism dissolves the measurement problem by making physics explicitly about the experiences of agents, not an objective external reality. Critics argue this makes physics solipsistic or circular. Defenders argue all other interpretations illegitimately assume an "objective" view from nowhere.', note: 'Radically agent-centred; dismisses "objective" wavefunction.' }
  }, ['copenhagen', 'mw', 'pilot', 'qbism']);

  // STANDARD MODEL GRID
  makeElemGrid('sm-grid', 'sm-detail', [
    { key: 'u', icon: 'u', label: 'Up quark', title: 'Up quark (u) — charge +2/3', body: 'One of the two light quarks that make up protons and neutrons. A proton is uud; a neutron is udd. Mass ~2.2 MeV/c². Quarks are confined — they cannot exist as free particles due to colour confinement: as two quarks are separated, the string of gluons between them gains energy faster than it takes to create a new quark-antiquark pair, so quark pairs pop out of the vacuum instead of the quarks separating.' },
    { key: 'd', icon: 'd', label: 'Down quark', title: 'Down quark (d) — charge −1/3', body: 'The other light quark. Mass ~4.7 MeV/c². Beta decay (n → p + e⁻ + νe) is a down quark converting to an up quark via the weak force: d → u + W⁻, W⁻ → e⁻ + anti-νe. This is the process that makes unstable nuclei radioactive.' },
    { key: 'e', icon: 'e⁻', label: 'Electron', title: 'Electron (e⁻) — charge −1', body: 'The lightest charged lepton, mass 0.511 MeV/c². Electrons determine the chemical properties of atoms (QM energy levels), carry current in conductors, and are responsible for all chemistry, biology, and technology. The positron (anti-electron) was the first antiparticle discovered (Anderson, 1932). Electron-positron annihilation produces two 511 keV gamma photons — exploited in PET scans.' },
    { key: 'nu', icon: 'ν', label: 'Neutrino', title: 'Electron neutrino (νe) — charge 0', body: 'Neutrinos interact only via the weak force and gravity. They pass through matter almost completely — a light-year of lead would stop about half the neutrinos passing through it. ~100 billion solar neutrinos pass through your thumbnail every second. Neutrinos were long thought massless; the observation of neutrino oscillations (1998, Nobel 2015) proved they have tiny but non-zero masses. The mass scale is ≲0.1 eV — about a million times less than the electron.' },
    { key: 'photon', icon: 'γ', label: 'Photon', title: 'Photon (γ) — force carrier of electromagnetism', body: 'The quantum of the electromagnetic field. Massless, spin-1, travels at c. All electromagnetic interactions (electric and magnetic forces, light, chemical bonds, contact forces) are mediated by photon exchange. Virtual photons — off-shell excitations with imaginary mass — mediate the force between charged particles. Real photons are the excitations you detect with your eyes and instruments.' },
    { key: 'gluon', icon: 'g', label: 'Gluon', title: 'Gluon (g) — force carrier of the strong force', body: 'Gluons mediate the strong nuclear force (quantum chromodynamics, QCD). Unlike photons, gluons carry colour charge themselves, which means they interact with each other — this is why the strong force gets stronger with distance (confinement), the opposite of gravity and electromagnetism. There are 8 types of gluon. The hadron masses (proton: 938 MeV) are mostly gluon field energy, not quark mass: the quarks contribute only ~10 MeV.' },
    { key: 'w', icon: 'W±', label: 'W boson', title: 'W boson (W±) — weak force, charge-changing', body: 'The W⁺ and W⁻ bosons mediate the charged-current weak interaction — the one responsible for beta decay and the reactions that power the Sun. They are massive (80.4 GeV), which is why the weak force has such short range (~10⁻¹⁸ m). The W boson can change a quark\'s flavour (u↔d, c↔s, t↔b), making it the agent of radioactive decay. In 2022, CDF reported a W mass 7σ higher than the Standard Model prediction — an anomaly still under investigation.' },
    { key: 'z', icon: 'Z', label: 'Z boson', title: 'Z boson (Z⁰) — weak force, neutral current', body: 'The Z boson mediates the neutral-current weak interaction — it doesn\'t change particle flavour, but does produce parity violation. Mass 91.2 GeV. The W and Z bosons were predicted by Glashow, Salam, and Weinberg\'s electroweak unification theory (Nobel 1979) and discovered at CERN in 1983 (Nobel 1984). The Z boson line shape measurement gives the number of light neutrino families: exactly 3.' },
    { key: 'higgs', icon: 'H', label: 'Higgs', title: 'Higgs boson (H) — gives mass', body: 'The Higgs field permeates all of space. Particles acquire mass by interacting with it: the stronger the interaction, the heavier the particle. Photons don\'t interact with the Higgs at all — they are massless. The Higgs boson is the quantum of the Higgs field: a ripple in it. It was predicted in 1964 (Higgs, Brout, Englert) and discovered at CERN\'s LHC in 2012 (Nobel 2013). Mass: 125.1 GeV. It decays primarily to b-quark pairs (58%) and W boson pairs (21%).' }
