  // QUANTUM TECH EXPLORER
  makeExplorer('qtech-btns', 'qtech-panel', {
    laser: { label: 'Lasers', title: 'Lasers — stimulated emission and coherent light', body: 'Einstein predicted stimulated emission in 1917: a photon encountering an excited atom can trigger the release of an identical photon (same phase, frequency, direction). Population inversion — more atoms in the excited state than ground state, achieved by pumping energy in — makes stimulated emission dominate over absorption. The result is a coherent beam of light. Applications range from barcode scanners and fiber-optic communications (carrying the internet) to precision cutting in surgery and manufacturing, laser cooling of atoms to nano-Kelvin temperatures, and gravitational wave detection (LIGO uses 200kW of laser power in 4km arm cavities).', note: 'Theodore Maiman built the first working laser in 1960 using synthetic ruby.' },
    semi: { label: 'Semiconductors', title: 'Semiconductors — quantum mechanics enables computing', body: 'Band theory (a quantum result) explains why silicon conducts only when enough thermal or light energy kicks electrons from the valence band across the ~1.1eV bandgap to the conduction band. Doping with phosphorus (n-type) or boron (p-type) creates controlled conductivity. The p-n junction is the foundation of diodes and transistors. A MOSFET — the transistor in every chip — uses a gate voltage to control a channel of current quantum-mechanically. Today\'s most advanced chips have ~2nm features (containing billions of transistors on a fingernail), where quantum tunnelling across gate oxides starts to limit further miniaturisation.', note: 'Shockley, Bardeen, Brattain (1947) — Nobel Prize 1956.' },
    mri: { label: 'MRI', title: 'MRI — quantum mechanics in hospitals', body: 'Magnetic resonance imaging exploits nuclear spin. Hydrogen nuclei (protons) in the body precess around a strong magnetic field at the Larmor frequency (~64 MHz at 1.5 Tesla). A radio-frequency pulse tips the spins; as they relax back, they emit RF signals detected by the scanner. Different tissues relax at different rates (T1 and T2 relaxation times), creating contrast. Gradient fields encode spatial information. Unlike X-rays or CT, MRI uses no ionising radiation. A 3T clinical scanner uses about 14km of superconducting niobium-titanium wire, cooled to 4K by liquid helium. Functional MRI (fMRI) detects blood oxygenation changes as a proxy for brain activity.', note: '~40,000 MRI machines in service worldwide. Annual scans: >100 million.' },
    qc: { label: 'Quantum Computing', title: 'Quantum computing — state of play 2025', body: 'A qubit can be in superposition of |0⟩ and |1⟩, and qubits can be entangled. This enables parallel computation over exponentially many states simultaneously. Shor\'s algorithm (1994) factorises N-digit numbers in polynomial time (vs exponential classically). Grover\'s algorithm provides quadratic speedup for database search. The challenge is decoherence: qubits must be isolated from the environment while still being controlled. As of 2025, the largest systems have ~1000+ noisy physical qubits, but fault-tolerant logical qubits (requiring ~1000 physical qubits each) have been demonstrated only at very small scales. The era of practical quantum advantage is approaching but not yet here.', note: 'IBM, Google, IonQ, and Quantinuum lead development; Microsoft pursues topological qubits.' },
    crypto: { label: 'Quantum Crypto', title: 'Quantum cryptography — theoretically unbreakable', body: 'BB84 (Bennett & Brassard, 1984) is the first quantum key distribution protocol. Alice encodes bits in photon polarisations, randomly switching between two bases. Bob measures, also randomly. Any eavesdropping disturbs the quantum states (no-cloning theorem), leaving detectable errors. Alice and Bob can share a provably secure key over an authenticated classical channel. China demonstrated satellite-based QKD over 1200km (2017). Post-quantum cryptography (NIST standards finalised 2024) provides classical algorithms believed resistant to quantum attack — the most practical near-term solution for securing existing infrastructure.', note: 'CRYSTALS-Kyber (now FIPS 203) is the NIST post-quantum standard for key encapsulation.' }
  }, ['laser', 'semi', 'mri', 'qc', 'crypto']);

