  // FATE OF UNIVERSE EXPLORER
  makeExplorer('fate-btns', 'fate-panel', {
    heatdeath: { label: 'Heat Death', title: 'Heat Death — the most likely fate', body: 'If dark energy remains constant (a cosmological constant), the universe expands forever at an accelerating rate. Galaxies beyond our Local Group recede beyond our event horizon and become invisible. Over ~10¹⁴ years, stars exhaust their fuel. Over ~10⁴⁰ years, all matter decays or falls into black holes. Black holes evaporate via Hawking radiation (complete after ~10⁶⁶ to 10¹⁰⁶ years for the most massive). The universe approaches maximum entropy — a vast, cold, dark, near-uniform heat bath with no free energy to do work. This is the second law of thermodynamics taken to its ultimate conclusion.', note: 'Supported by current observations. The universe\'s fate depends entirely on the nature of dark energy.' },
    bigrip: { label: 'Big Rip', title: 'Big Rip — if dark energy strengthens', body: 'If dark energy is "phantom energy" (equation of state parameter w < −1), it grows stronger over time. Eventually the expansion rate accelerates so fast that it tears apart galactic clusters (~60 Gyr from now), then galaxies, then solar systems, planets, molecules, and finally atoms — all in the final minutes. Even the fabric of spacetime shreds. Current measurements of w are consistent with −1 (cosmological constant) but have error bars that don\'t rule out w < −1. The Big Rip is possible but not favoured by current data.', note: 'Requires phantom dark energy — not confirmed by current observations.' },
    bounce: { label: 'Big Bounce', title: 'Cyclic / Bouncing Cosmologies', body: 'In loop quantum cosmology, the quantum nature of spacetime prevents a true singularity. When the universe collapses (if it ever does), the Planck density is reached and quantum repulsion causes a bounce into a new expansion phase. In the Ekpyrotic / cyclic model (Steinhardt & Turok), two "brane" universes periodically collide, each collision producing a Big Bang-like event. These models struggle to explain why entropy is low in each new cycle (the entropy ratchet problem). Evidence is sparse — the CMB shows no confirmed imprints from pre-Big-Bang cycles, though some claim marginal signals.', note: 'Speculative; not ruled out; the entropy problem is severe.' }
